{
  "dom_id": "step-q164169",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14148803/assets/867fa59781c1732536e984398478996150c3d6b715c9d216bb82a35e0caf27e0.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global minimum points of the function?",
  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=-2.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-2.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(1,1),$ so the global minimum point is $(1,1,-2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14148803/assets/9e2c084b2697ada9a957eec3629ac71aea150563a3af37b4f5d0b25e1921559a.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
